  {
    "title": "-yor never changes, but the vowel before it does",
    "body": "The present tense ending -yor is always “yor”, even after front vowels. The vowel before it follows harmony (i, ı, ü or u) and takes the stress.",
    "pattern": "[ıiuü]yor",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "geliyor",
        "say": "geh-LEE-yor",
        "en": "is coming"
      },
      {
        "word": "gidiyor",
        "say": "gee-DEE-yor",
        "en": "is going"
      },
      {
        "word": "okuyor",
        "say": "oh-KOO-yor",
        "en": "is reading"
      },
      {
        "word": "görüyor",
        "say": "gö-RÜ-yor",
        "en": "is seeing"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Double consonants are held longer",
    "body": "A doubled letter is held for twice as long, the way English joins “t” in “hot tea”. anne is “ahn-NEH”, not “AH-neh”.",
    "pattern": "([bcçdfgğhjklmnprsştvyz])\\1",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "anne",
        "say": "ahn-NEH",
        "en": "mother"
      },
      {
        "word": "elli",
        "say": "el-LEE",
        "en": "fifty"
      },
