        "word": "etti",
        "say": "et-TEE",
        "en": "did, made"
      },
      {
        "word": "dikkat",
        "say": "deek-KAHT",
        "en": "attention, careful"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "p, ç, t, k at the end turn into b, c, d, ğ before a vowel",
    "body": "Turkish words rarely end in b, c, d or g. Many words ending in p, ç, t or k soften that sound when an ending starting with a vowel is added, and the spelling shows it: kitap but kitabı, çocuk but çocuğu.",
    "pattern": "(?<!\\p{L})(?:kitab|sahib|hesab|grub|kulüb|ekib|mektub|cevab|dolab|kebab|ceb|sebeb|ağac|ihtiyac|ilac|kâğıd|vücud|umud|gid|çocuğ|yemeğ|ekmeğ|köpeğ|sokağ|mutfağ|yatağ|durağ|bardağ|ocağ|uçağ|bacağ|tabağ|toprağ|ayağ)(?=[aeıioöuü])|(?<=\\p{L}[ae]c[ae])ğ(?=[aeıioöuü])|(?<=\\p{L}(?:[dt][ıiuü]|l[ıiuü]))ğ(?=[aeıioöuü])",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "kitabı",
        "say": "kee-tah-BUH",
        "en": "the book (kitap)"
      },
      {
        "word": "ağacı",
        "say": "aa-ah-JUH",
        "en": "the tree (ağaç)"
      },
      {
        "word": "çocuğu",
        "say": "choh-joo-OO",
        "en": "his or her child (çocuk)"
      },
      {
        "word": "gidiyor",
        "say": "gee-DEE-yor",
        "en": "is going (git)"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "n before b or p sounds like “m”",
    "body": "Before b or p, also across a word boundary, n is said with closed lips as “m”. İstanbul is “ees-TAHM-bool” and on beş is “om-BESH”.",
    "pattern": "n(?= ?[bp])",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "on beş",
        "say": "om-BESH",
        "en": "fifteen"
      },
      {
        "word": "İstanbul",
        "say": "ees-TAHM-bool",
        "en": "Istanbul"
      },
      {
        "word": "zeytinyağlı",
        "say": "zey-teen-yaa-LUH",
        "en": "cooked in olive oil"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "An apostrophe joins a name to its ending",
    "body": "Endings on names are split off with an apostrophe, but you read straight through it. The ending follows the name's vowels and the stress stays on the name: İzmir'de is “EEZ-meer-deh”.",
    "pattern": "(?<=\\p{L})['’](?=\\p{L})",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "İzmir'de",
        "say": "EEZ-meer-deh",
        "en": "in Izmir"
      },
      {
        "word": "Ankara'ya",
        "say": "AHN-kah-rah-yah",
        "en": "to Ankara"
      },
      {
        "word": "Türkiye'de",
        "say": "TÜR-kee-yeh-deh",
        "en": "in Turkey"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ğ between a, ı, o, u is silent",
    "body": "Between two back vowels, ğ disappears and the vowels run together. Two different vowels glide into each other, sometimes with a light “w” between o or u; two of the same make one long vowel.",
    "pattern": "(?<=[aıou])ğ(?=[aıou])",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "doğum",
        "say": "doh-OOM",
        "en": "birth"
      },
      {
        "word": "soğuk",
        "say": "soh-OOK",
        "en": "cold"
      },
      {
        "word": "ağaç",
        "say": "aa-AHCH",
        "en": "tree"
      },
      {
        "word": "çocuğu",
        "say": "choh-joo-OO",
        "en": "his or her child"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "ğ between e, i, ö, ü is a soft “y”",
    "body": "Between two front vowels, ğ is a weak “y” glide or nothing at all, so değil can sound like “deh-YEEL” or “deel”. Never make it a “g”.",
    "pattern": "(?<=[eiöü])ğ(?=[eiöü])",
    "examples": [
